WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.580
$30 shingle bundles hold a couple dollars of materials, and that gap is the roofing opening. This is First Principles Daily, episode 138. The price against the three materials, the additions since 1903 that locked the sequence, a redesign that would drop those additions, and the principal underneath. A finished bundle reaches the job site priced at several times the fiberglass mat, the coating asphalt, and the ceramic granules inside it. The difference does not come from exotic raw inputs.

00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:33.960
It comes from how the product is colored, packaged, warranted, and installed by crews

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:36.100
that also handle tar off and disposal.

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:40.900
That spread is a clear opening for anyone willing to rebuild the weather barrier from

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:44.840
the materials upward rather than from the current supply chain downward.

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:50.320
Asphalt shingles reach their present form through incremental additions, not a wholesale

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:50.980
redesign.

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:56.280
Henry Reynolds of Grand Rapids introduced the first asphalt shingles in 1903.

00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:02.680
granules surfacing followed a few years later to improve durability. Manufacturers later shifted

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:08.280
from organic felt mats to fiberglass, which now forms the structural core of nearly every North

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:14.240
American shingle. The fiberglass mat itself is a small fraction of total weight, yet it supplies

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:19.680
most of the tear resistance. Once the mat is saturated with asphalt and coated with granules,

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:24.320
the product moves through separate finishing lines that create dozens of color blends for

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:30.160
different regional tastes. Each blend needs its own inventory, its own packaging, and its own

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:35.840
warranty registration system. Roofing crews then remove the old roof, dispose of the waste, and

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:41.540
install the new shingles one course at a time, often under tight weather windows. Those steps

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:47.540
compound. Color Variety drives proliferation of stock keeping units. Bundle packaging protects

00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:48.960
tax against handling damage.

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:53.180
On-site labor absorbs the cost of mismatched or damaged material.

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:58.400
Building codes and manufacture instructions further lock in under-layment layers and

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:01.080
fastening patterns that crews must follow exactly.

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:06.440
The price feels ordinary inside the industry because every participant has optimized around

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:10.000
the existing sequence rather than questioning the sequence itself.

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:16.140
On the line, the fiberglass mat is fed continuously into a saturated tank of hot asphalt that

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:21.600
coats the fibers. Ceramic coated mineral granules are then pressed onto the top surface for

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:27.120
ultraviolet protection. The back receives sand or slag finds, so the finished sheets do

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:32.380
not stick together in storage. Each coating and granule step runs on long production lines

00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:37.860
that must be cleaned and reset whenever a color blend changes. That reset adds downtime

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:43.200
and material waste later priced into every bundle. After the sheets are cut into individual

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:48.520
shingles and stacked, they move into regional distribution centers that hold dozens of color-stock

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:53.740
keeping units to match local architectural preferences. When a contractor orders a job,

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:59.440
the bundles travel to the site along with separate roles of underlayment. Those roles must also

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:03.960
meet American society for testing and material standards for secondary water resistance.

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:10.040
On the roof, the old shingles are stripped, the deck is inspected, and new underlayment is rolled

00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:14.640
out before the first course of shingles is nailed. Every one of those hand operations

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:20.240
happens under variable weather and roof geometry. Labor hours and disposal fees become part

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:24.180
of the delivered price even though none of them changes the three core materials that

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:29.612
actually keep water out. If those raw materials could be arranged into finished shingles at

00:03:29.612 --> 00:03:35.452
no cost beyond their commodity value, the floor would be set by three inputs. The fiberglass

00:03:35.452 --> 00:03:40.852
Matt is glass fibers plus a binder, and its commodity contribution per bundle is modest.

00:03:41.552 --> 00:03:46.752
The asphalt, satirant, and coating is a petroleum-derived product whose price tracks crude oil

00:03:46.752 --> 00:03:47.172
markets.

00:03:47.852 --> 00:03:52.812
The mineral granules are crushed rock with a ceramic coating, again a bulk commodity.

00:03:53.492 --> 00:03:58.552
Add the three together and a rough magic wand estimate lands at $1 to $3 per bundle,

00:03:59.092 --> 00:04:01.692
once transport and basic processing are included.

00:04:02.232 --> 00:04:04.272
That estimate is the theoretical floor.

00:04:04.272 --> 00:04:08.472
4. The price of the stuff itself, if forming it into a shingle, were free.

00:04:09.112 --> 00:04:13.692
Published retail and contractor prices for standard three-tab or architectural shingles

00:04:13.692 --> 00:04:19.132
commonly fall between $25 and $40 per bundle, depending on region and volume.

00:04:19.832 --> 00:04:23.992
Divide a finished price in that band by a material floor of $1 to $3.

00:04:24.372 --> 00:04:29.152
The quotient, the idiot index, lands in a range of roughly 10 to 25.

00:04:29.152 --> 00:04:34.572
A bundle can cost something like 10 to 25 times the commodity value of what is in it.

00:04:34.972 --> 00:04:39.852
That is an estimate from public price bands and a rough material floor, not a measured

00:04:39.852 --> 00:04:44.132
factory audit, and the listener can rerun the division with any local quote.

00:04:44.552 --> 00:04:46.732
The gap lives in several distinct places.

00:04:47.292 --> 00:04:52.032
Color matching lines and the inventory carrying costs that go with them add several dollars.

00:04:52.552 --> 00:04:56.312
Bundle packaging, palatizing, and regional distribution add more.

00:04:56.912 --> 00:05:01.792
Warranty registration systems and the administrative overhead they require sit on top of that.

00:05:02.312 --> 00:05:07.292
The largest single addition comes from tar off, disposal, and hand installation by specialized

00:05:07.292 --> 00:05:07.812
crews.

00:05:08.412 --> 00:05:12.892
Their productivity is limited by weather, roof pitch, and the need to coordinate with other

00:05:12.892 --> 00:05:13.332
trades.

00:05:13.912 --> 00:05:17.212
None of these steps is required by the physics of keeping water out.

00:05:17.752 --> 00:05:21.672
Each is required by the way the product has been packaged and sold for decades.

00:05:21.672 --> 00:05:27.272
The matte, although less than 2% of finished single weight, carries the tensile loads that

00:05:27.272 --> 00:05:29.032
prevent tearing over nail heads.

00:05:29.532 --> 00:05:32.612
The asphalt supplies waterproofing and granule adhesion.

00:05:33.132 --> 00:05:37.912
The granule's block ultraviolet light that would otherwise embrittle the asphalt, because

00:05:37.912 --> 00:05:40.912
those three functions can be satisfied with commodity inputs.

00:05:41.512 --> 00:05:45.852
Any price above a few dollars per bundle has to be explained by the sequence of operations

00:05:45.852 --> 00:05:49.232
after material formation, not by the materials themselves.

00:05:49.232 --> 00:05:49.572
cells.

00:05:50.212 --> 00:05:54.872
Color campaigns require separate granule hoppers and line cleanouts that reduce throughput.

00:05:55.572 --> 00:06:00.352
Each additional stock keeping unit multiplies warehouse space and the risk of unsold inventory

00:06:00.352 --> 00:06:02.912
that must eventually be discounted or scrapped.

00:06:03.472 --> 00:06:08.432
On-site, the crew must handle, align, and fasten thousands of individual shingles, while

00:06:08.432 --> 00:06:13.172
also managing underlayment seams and flashing details inspected for code compliance.

00:06:13.732 --> 00:06:18.792
Those labor and logistics layers sit on top of the material cost, and they remain even

00:06:18.792 --> 00:06:23.941
even when raw material prices fluctuate. A redesign would start by collapsing color

00:06:23.941 --> 00:06:28.361
variety into a smaller set of blends that can be produced continuously rather than in

00:06:28.361 --> 00:06:33.161
separate campaigns. Continuous production of a limited palette would eliminate repeated

00:06:33.161 --> 00:06:38.421
line changeovers and the carrying cost of dozens of color inventories. The next move would

00:06:38.421 --> 00:06:44.001
shift from loose shingles to larger pre-sealed panels or rolls. Larger pieces cut the number

00:06:44.001 --> 00:06:48.681
of field joints and they cut the labor hours needed to make those joints. They would also

00:06:48.681 --> 00:06:53.541
cut the number of nail lines and overlap seams that crews must execute on a sloped surface.

00:06:54.261 --> 00:06:58.681
Underlayment and shingle layers could be integrated into a single factory applied assembly

00:06:58.681 --> 00:07:04.601
that meets code requirements without a separate site installation. Factory lamination of underlayment

00:07:04.601 --> 00:07:09.361
to the shingle layer would remove one entire site visit and the weather exposure window that

00:07:09.361 --> 00:07:14.421
visit creates. Such a system would still need to satisfy wind uplift and fire ratings.

00:07:14.421 --> 00:07:19.241
Those tests could be run on the integrated product rather than on individual components.

00:07:19.681 --> 00:07:24.301
Each of these moves requires that the new assembly still pass the same American society

00:07:24.301 --> 00:07:29.701
for testing in materials, wind and fire tests that individual shingles now meet, and that

00:07:29.701 --> 00:07:32.801
code officials recognize the integrated product as equivalent.

00:07:33.481 --> 00:07:34.861
The hard constraints are real.

00:07:35.581 --> 00:07:38.081
Local building departments must accept the new assembly.

00:07:38.781 --> 00:07:40.281
Ensures must price the risk.

00:07:40.281 --> 00:07:42.901
Crews must be retrained or displaced.

00:07:43.601 --> 00:07:44.741
Scale would also matter.

00:07:45.141 --> 00:07:49.901
A single regional plant could not amortize the tooling, while national distribution would

00:07:49.901 --> 00:07:53.601
reintroduce some of the inventory problems the redesign aims to remove.

00:07:54.361 --> 00:07:59.221
The capital cost of wider production lines, and the need for regional manufacturing footprints

00:07:59.221 --> 00:08:01.981
to keep shipping distances short, are genuine limits.

00:08:02.641 --> 00:08:06.741
Those limits would have to be solved before the material floor becomes the dominant term

00:08:06.741 --> 00:08:07.821
in the delivered price.

00:08:07.821 --> 00:08:13.541
If the hurdles were cleared, the cost trajectory would be set by how far the idiot index could

00:08:13.541 --> 00:08:18.561
be driven toward that material floor, not by incremental improvements in granule adhesion

00:08:18.561 --> 00:08:19.641
or sealant strips.

00:08:20.579 --> 00:08:25.499
One principle is that variety introduced after the material stage multiplies every downstream

00:08:25.499 --> 00:08:28.499
cost without changing the weather barrier function.

00:08:29.179 --> 00:08:31.239
A new color is not a new physics problem.

00:08:31.739 --> 00:08:36.759
It is a new hopper, a new clean out, a new warehouse slot, a new warranty record, and

00:08:36.759 --> 00:08:39.379
And a new chance that the wrong blend shows up on a truck.

00:08:40.079 --> 00:08:42.139
None of that changes how water is kept out.

00:08:42.699 --> 00:08:46.999
Another principle is that labor performed on site after the product leaves the factory

00:08:46.999 --> 00:08:50.099
tends to embed the largest and most persistent markups.

00:08:50.679 --> 00:08:55.439
Terror, disposal, rolling underlayment, and nailing one course at a time are priced

00:08:55.439 --> 00:08:58.739
into the roof even when asphalt and granules get cheaper.

00:08:59.319 --> 00:09:04.419
The same logic that once asked how many separate valves a rocket engine truly needs, now

00:09:04.419 --> 00:09:08.219
Now asks how many separate color runs and crew visits a roof truly needs.

00:09:08.879 --> 00:09:14.359
The weather barrier is three commodity jobs, tensile strength in a thin mat, water proofing

00:09:14.359 --> 00:09:17.299
in asphalt, and ultraviolet blocking in granules.

00:09:17.839 --> 00:09:22.019
Everything after that is a choice about how the industry chose to sell and place those

00:09:22.019 --> 00:09:22.439
jobs.

00:09:23.039 --> 00:09:27.399
Who will run the first full-scale trial that treats the entire roof assembly as a single

00:09:27.399 --> 00:09:30.559
manufactured weather barrier, rather than a kit of parts?

00:09:31.179 --> 00:09:33.199
That's first principles daily for today.

00:09:33.199 --> 00:09:37.759
If this changed how you see what things cost, share it with someone who'd appreciate

00:09:37.759 --> 00:09:39.859
it, and subscribe wherever you listen.

